WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:06.740
Let's get started.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.760
So before proceeding
with today's lecture,

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.860
I just wanted to orient since
we're a little bit since we're

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.000
at the end of the quarter now.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.070
So this is a final lecture
that either I or Tatsu

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:19.980
will be giving.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:23.480
So now, let's begin
today's lecture.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.230
So last time, Tatsu
gave an overview of RL

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.360
from verifiable
rewards and he talked

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.280
about policy gradient
algorithms such as PPO and GRPO.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:37.220
This lecture, we're
going to deep dive

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:40.170
into the mechanics of
policy gradient GRPO.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.040
So we won't necessarily
cover new material,

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.000
but just to take the existing
material and go a bit deeper

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.320
and show you code and
some math as well.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:57.510
So just to remind
us what we're doing.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.310
So we're doing reinforcement
for language models,

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:03.090
and in reinforcement
learning you

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:06.010
have to define the states and
actions and rewards and so on.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:09.030
And just to be very clear,
I think this hopefully

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:10.450
should be obvious by now.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.390
But just to state the obvious.

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:17.280
So the state is the
prompt plus the response

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.640
that you've generated so far.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.810
And each action is generating
a particular token,

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:23.910
the next token.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:28.570
The reward is essentially
how good the response is.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:31.450
And for the purpose
of this class,

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.320
we're going to focus on
outcome rewards, where

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.800
the reward is a function
of the entire response.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.070
We're going to focus on
rewards that are verifiable.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.570
So the computation
is deterministic.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:45.420
Some function just
returns a reward

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.900
as opposed to having
to ask a human.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.620
And in this setting,
typically, in reinforcement

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:56.010
learning you see notions of
discounting and bootstrapping

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.650
where it's multiple turns
where you're getting.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:03.060
And that really makes sense when
you have rewards in the middle.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.010
If this is more of you're just
generally a bunch of actions

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.449
and you see if your
answer is correct.

00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:12.080
So that obviously
introduces a bunch

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:16.080
of difficulties because it's
very sparse and delayed reward.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.880
But it also simplifies, I think,
some of the conceptual thinking

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:21.260
around this.

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:25.140
So for example, you might
have a question math problem.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:28.800
And then there's a chain of
thought you rethink for a while.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.238
And then finally, the
language model might generate.

00:02:32.238 --> 00:02:33.780
Therefore, the answer
is three miles.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.470
And then the reward would have
to extract the three miles

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:39.950
and see if that matches
some ground truth.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:43.220
So in reinforcement
learning, typically people

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.160
talk about the
transition dynamics.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:49.730
It's very important in this
case in the language model

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:51.420
setting it's very simple.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:53.340
It's just the plus operation.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:58.350
You just append the
action into the state.

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:01.050
This has a number of
important implications

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:03.750
because you can--
because you understand

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.260
the dynamics of the world.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:08.740
You can do planning
test time compute.

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:11.250
And this is something that
roboticists would only

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:14.400
dream of if they had a
transition model, dynamics

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.290
of the world, they
could simulate

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.690
and you could do
all these things.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.410
In language modeling,
we can do that,

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:23.880
but not true in general
reinforcement learning.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:27.840
And the other thing to realize
is that the notion of state

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.630
is really made up.

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:32.520
If you think about
reinforcement learning

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.680
in the context of
robotics, the state

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.980
is the joint angles, the
position, maybe an image,

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:38.590
or something.

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:40.930
It's really much grounded
in the real-world.

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:42.960
Whereas language
models the state

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.660
is just tokens that the language
model happens to generate.

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:51.420
And this introduces
a lot of flexibility

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.390
and introduces a lot of degrees
of freedom, where the language

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:56.990
model can make up
its own scratchpad

00:03:56.990 --> 00:03:58.830
in order to arrive
at the final answer.

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:01.250
Whereas often a robot
is very much limited

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:03.860
by the external world
in terms of what

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.210
states are even achievable.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.180
So the issue here
isn't about reaching

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:11.840
any state, which is often
the challenge in robotics

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:12.340
and control.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:13.632
You can't even reach the state.

00:04:13.632 --> 00:04:16.160
Here you can reach any state
you want by just writing down

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.600
tokens, but the
challenge is making sure

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:23.580
that these tokens actually
lead to some ground truth

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:26.120
answer that's correct.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:28.980
So I think it's
worth reflecting on.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:30.630
It's all reinforcement learning.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:33.560
But the intuitions
and what's hard

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.580
and what's easy changes in
the language modeling setting.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:41.720
So the policy in this case is
just a language model action

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:45.080
conditioned on the state, which
is the prompt and response given

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:48.290
so far, this is often fine tuned
from an existing pre-trained

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:49.970
language model.

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:53.900
And then there's multiple
names for this rollout episode

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.000
trajectory you.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.700
In this outcome reward setting,
you have to start with a state.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:02.100
You generate a bunch of
actions, a.k.a. tokens.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.650
You get a single number,
which is a reward.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:08.360
And then your objective is to
maximize the expected reward

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:11.180
where the expectation is
taken over prompts which

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:13.940
the environment gives
you, and also the response

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:19.760
tokens which your policy pi that
you're optimizing gives you.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:23.150
So hopefully, that
makes everything

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:25.443
very grounded and precise.

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:33.260
Let me if you have any
questions about this setup.

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:37.670
So let's now talk about
policy gradient, which

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:42.585
is a whole class of methods
for learning the policy, which

00:05:42.585 --> 00:05:44.210
essentially start
with a policy and try

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:47.960
to improve it using
gradient methods.

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:49.760
So for notational
simplicity, we're

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:53.110
just going to let a denote
the entire response instead

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:55.780
of a single action.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:58.708
Because we're in the
outcome reward setting,

00:05:58.708 --> 00:05:59.750
it doesn't really matter.

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:01.480
You can just think
about the actions

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:05.590
as being all generated at
once by the language models.

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:09.100
And then you get a
single reward at the end.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:12.550
So remember, we want to maximize
the expected reward with respect

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:14.120
to the policy pi.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:18.490
So just expected reward is
integral over the distribution

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:22.540
over states times policy
of action, sequence

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:24.760
given state times the reward.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:26.480
So this is pretty
straightforward.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:29.238
And policy gradient says
do the obvious thing.

00:06:29.238 --> 00:06:30.530
You want to optimize something.

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:31.580
Let's take the gradient.

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:35.260
So you start taking
the gradient.

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:39.890
So the gradient only
touches the policy.

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:45.490
And this is key trick.

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:49.580
It's like the policy gradient
theorem, some people call it.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:53.540
But it's really just
the chain rule for log.

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:57.380
So this basically,
gets rewritten

00:06:57.380 --> 00:07:00.600
as a pi and then the
gradient of the log.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:04.440
And then I'm going to rewrite
it in terms of expectation.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:08.420
So expectation is hiding
basically the distribution

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:10.560
over SNA here.

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:12.990
So this form should
be very familiar.

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:16.140
So this is the gradient
of the expected reward.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:18.890
So naive policy
gradient basically says,

00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:22.850
I'm just going to sample
a prompt from P, sample

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:25.310
response from A,
and then I'm just

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:27.890
going to update the
parameters based on whatever

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:29.930
inside this expectation.

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:33.440
So think about if
you're doing SGD,

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:39.000
you look at the expected
loss in your sample term

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:43.340
and you just evaluate
and go with that.

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:49.440
So interpretation, this is
essentially the same as S of t

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:53.530
except for everything is
weighted by the reward.

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:56.680
So an S of t a human being
writes down A for you,

00:07:56.680 --> 00:08:00.570
and you just mimic A and update
the parameters to maximize

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:02.050
the probability of that action.

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:04.600
And now, A is
generated by the model.

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:07.740
And now-- but you get
to hit it with a reward.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:11.260
So just as a intuition building.

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:13.390
So suppose that the
reward is either 0 or 1.

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:15.030
Is it correct or wrong?

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:18.840
So in this case, the
naive policy gradient

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:21.910
is just going to only update
on the correct responses.

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:24.630
So R is going to be 0
for the actions that

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:25.870
lead to incorrect responses.

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:27.703
So those are going to
be completely ignored.

00:08:27.703 --> 00:08:30.340
And then on the correct
responses R is 1.

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:33.030
So you just
essentially sample, you

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:35.309
select the responses
that are correct,

00:08:35.309 --> 00:08:38.159
and you just do S of t on this.

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:42.460
The only difference is that the
data set is changing over time,

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:46.980
because after you do an update,
now, you have a new policy

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:49.460
and you generate the data
set based on that policy

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:51.020
and you iterate and so on.

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:55.060
But this is very natural.

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:59.440
And the only challenge here is--

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:00.470
not the only challenge.

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:02.800
The main challenge
is that people

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:04.960
talk about policy
gradient as being

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:07.820
high noise or high variance.

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:09.920
So in stochastic
gradient descent,

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:11.420
if you're doing
supervised learning,

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:15.460
this is also can be the
case that your gradient is

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:17.200
very noisy.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:20.470
And typically, if you
use larger batch sizes

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:25.580
and just keep on going, then it
empirically seems to work out.

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:28.360
Reinforcement learning
is a whole other level

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:32.680
in terms of noise and variance
that you have to deal with.

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:36.910
So in particular, if
you look at this setting

00:09:36.910 --> 00:09:40.760
where you have 0
or 1 as a reward,

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:42.950
think about solving
a hard math problem.

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:48.000
So in this setting is a
sparse rewards, the rewards

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:51.090
are sparse, which means
that very few responses are

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:53.140
going to get reward one.

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:55.000
Most are going to get 0.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:57.480
So if you have a bad
policy and you roll out

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:01.740
and you generate your
actions, most of your rewards

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:03.010
are going to be 0.

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:05.200
Which means if you
look at the gradient,

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:09.010
you're basically making
a 0 gradient update.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:10.950
So if you're in a setting
where your policy is

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:14.260
so bad that you're not
able to get reward at all,

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:16.270
then guess what?

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:20.410
You're not making any gradient
updates and your stuck.

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:23.335
So that's a pretty
bad setting to be in.

00:10:23.335 --> 00:10:24.960
At least in supervised
learning, you're

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:26.830
at least making an
update towards something.

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:29.080
You're guaranteed to make
an update towards something.

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:32.520
But in reinforcement
learning and sparse rewards,

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:34.590
that's not true anymore.

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:35.700
Question?

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:39.390
Why is the data set
changing over time?

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:42.760
So the question is, why is the
data set changing over time?

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:44.830
So if you think about--

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:46.820
this is your objective function.

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:48.620
So you're going to optimize.

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:51.640
So you iterate each
iteration you're

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:56.450
going to compute the gradient
and then update the parameters.

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:59.110
So the gradient--
computing the gradient

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:07.780
involves sampling a
response and then updating.

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:09.920
So now, your parameters
are different.

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:13.630
So the next time you sample
response it's going to--

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:17.960
might be different now because
it's coming from the new model.

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:21.670
And if you sample multiple
responses those responses that's

00:11:21.670 --> 00:11:24.640
going to be a different data set
from a different distribution

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:26.410
than where you started.

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:29.630
So your data set hopefully, is
going to get better over time.

00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:33.110
So intuitively, even if you
have this sparse reward,

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:37.780
as long as you can get some
of the easy questions correct

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:40.390
and you can get some reward,
you can update your model,

00:11:40.390 --> 00:11:43.550
and you hope that the
model can generalize

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:44.760
beyond those examples.

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:46.820
So that next
iteration, when you're

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:50.520
sampling and solving a slightly
more difficult questions,

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:53.540
you're going to get a
bunch of responses that

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:55.290
happen to get the right answer.

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:59.300
And now, your data set will
have higher and higher reward

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:00.070
over time.

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:08.570
So this is in contrast
to reinforcement learning

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:11.280
with human feedback where
you use pairwise preferences,

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:13.050
you estimate a reward model.

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:17.390
And the reward model is
generally a bit more continuous.

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:19.820
It's going to assign
essentially a numerical value

00:12:19.820 --> 00:12:21.750
to every response
how good it is.

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:26.840
This is quite different
than the verifiable reward

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:31.310
setting where it's
correct or incorrect.

00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:34.490
And notice that, I mean,
I'm emphasizing this point

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:36.150
that it's all
reinforcement learning.

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:38.850
It's all even within the
scope of language model.

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:41.460
Sometimes you even use the
same algorithms like PPO,

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:44.280
but the intuitions for--

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:48.810
and for maybe how you set
the hyperparameters will--

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:52.240
and how you tune the
model will be different.

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:54.630
So I noticed that the
rewards are sparse.

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:57.850
So you don't update the
parameters if the reward is 0.

00:12:57.850 --> 00:12:58.570
Is that correct?

00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:00.570
Then should we just
set it to negative 1,

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:03.700
so it goes the other direction
so we can use those rewards?

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:04.200
Yeah.

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:08.788
So the question is-- well, in
this case if the reward is 0,

00:13:08.788 --> 00:13:10.330
then you don't update
the parameters.

00:13:10.330 --> 00:13:13.720
Why not make the
reward like minus 1?

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:16.380
So at least you're
pushing the model away

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:18.010
from those incorrect responses.

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:21.120
This is something that
will happen automatically

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:23.890
when we look at baselines.

00:13:23.890 --> 00:13:29.130
Because there-- this is just
a simple naive policy gradient

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:30.880
where you're updating
against the rewards.

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:33.422
We're going to show that there's
much better things than just

00:13:33.422 --> 00:13:36.390
using the reward.

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:36.932
Question?

00:13:36.932 --> 00:13:38.640
I think question--
and then I think there

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:39.790
was a question over there.

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:45.450
--the reward, only when we
got the following response.

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:48.320
The last word of the response.

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:51.800
So the reward is a
function of the prompt

00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:53.540
and the response,
the whole response.

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:56.060
And the response is
correct or not only

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:58.080
when we generate it completely.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:03.630
So we have gradient only
on the last token or?

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:06.170
So yeah-- so you're
getting at sometimes

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:08.790
you have reward before
you even finish.

00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:13.520
And so the other type of reward
is called process supervision

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:17.570
or process rewards which
allow you to basically keep

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:19.910
track of how well you're doing.

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:22.590
There are things
you can do there.

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:26.450
And generally, that's better
if your process reward is good.

00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:29.610
For simplicity, now, I'm just
assuming it's an outcome reward.

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:32.540
But absolutely, if you
have reward earlier,

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:34.700
you should always evaluate
the reward as early

00:14:34.700 --> 00:14:37.170
as you can if you trust it.

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:40.170
The problem with process reward
in language models that you just

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:40.780
don't--

00:14:40.780 --> 00:14:43.290
it's really hard to get
a good process reward

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:49.120
because unless you're
completely going off the rails,

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:52.805
it's hard to tell how
well you're doing.

00:14:55.950 --> 00:14:57.570
Is there another question?

00:14:57.570 --> 00:14:58.210
Same question.

00:14:58.210 --> 00:15:00.790
What's the reward model here
that we are working with?

00:15:00.790 --> 00:15:03.775
So how are we getting 0 and 1.

00:15:03.775 --> 00:15:05.650
So the question is, what
is the reward model?

00:15:05.650 --> 00:15:07.770
I'll show you some
examples of this.

00:15:07.770 --> 00:15:10.500
But you can think about
the reward model which

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:13.320
I think for your assignment
you're implementing basically

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:15.960
the language models
generate some stuff

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:18.060
and then you parse
the answer out

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:21.660
and you check the answer
against your answer key

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:24.460
and you return 1 if it
matches and 0 otherwise.

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:33.060
So maximize expected
reward, policy gradient

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:34.780
does the obvious thing.

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:40.000
Naive policy gradient--
sorry, does the obvious thing.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:43.250
But you have this high
noise and variance problem.

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:49.240
So the-- a key idea in
policy gradient methods

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:52.090
is using what is
called a baseline.

00:15:52.090 --> 00:15:55.400
And so we recall that this
is the expected reward.

00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:57.880
If you could actually
compute this expected reward

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:00.400
you'd be pretty good.

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:02.990
Because then you could
just compute that.

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:05.870
But you can't.

00:16:05.870 --> 00:16:10.090
So you instead use
unbiased estimate

00:16:10.090 --> 00:16:13.210
which usually means ripping
off the expectation,

00:16:13.210 --> 00:16:16.190
sampling of and ripping
off the expectation.

00:16:16.190 --> 00:16:21.430
This is an unbiased
estimate, but its variance

00:16:21.430 --> 00:16:23.960
can be pretty high.

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:27.040
So just to give a toy
example to appreciate

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:28.700
what I mean by high variance.

00:16:28.700 --> 00:16:31.120
So suppose you have
just two states, so two

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:33.590
prompts if you want, S1 and S2.

00:16:33.590 --> 00:16:36.690
And from S1 you have
two possible actions.

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:38.460
A1 gives you a reward 11.

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:40.410
A2 gives you a reward 9.

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:44.030
And from state S2,
a1 gives you reward

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:47.360
0 and A2 gives you reward two.

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:51.470
So S1 fundamentally,
gives you higher reward

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:53.880
and S2 just gives
you lower reward.

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:55.520
And of course, the
best policy would

00:16:55.520 --> 00:17:01.190
be in S1 you want to choose A1
and S2 you want to choose A2.

00:17:01.190 --> 00:17:06.079
So you don't want S1 to--

00:17:06.079 --> 00:17:09.200
and going to A2.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:17.000
But you notice that this 9 is
actually larger than this S2.

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:20.180
So you can end up
in situations where

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:24.710
each state think about this as a
really S1 as being a really easy

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:25.500
question.

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:29.690
And S2 is maybe a
harder question.

00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:33.740
So you just because
something gets

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:38.440
high reward doesn't mean you
absolutely should be updating

00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:39.980
aggressively against it.

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:43.150
So if 9 is greater
than 2-- so if you're

00:17:43.150 --> 00:17:48.590
doing naive gradient updates,
you might pick S1 going to A2.

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:49.970
And it's like wow this is great.

00:17:49.970 --> 00:17:53.120
I'm going to get 9
times the gradient.

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:59.500
And then I look at S2, A2 and I
say well that's only reward two.

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:03.790
So I'm pushing up that
less when in fact,

00:18:03.790 --> 00:18:05.860
if you look at the
whole picture it's

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:09.130
clear that that's
maybe just locally not

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:10.460
doesn't really make sense.

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:12.890
I mean, I would stress
that in expectation.

00:18:12.890 --> 00:18:15.920
If you look at overall states
and actions, it all works out.

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:17.210
The math all works out.

00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:24.590
But you have to look at
everything to get that.

00:18:24.590 --> 00:18:26.085
Does this example make sense?

00:18:28.990 --> 00:18:31.970
Why would we use A2
in the first case?

00:18:31.970 --> 00:18:34.400
Because A1 already has 11.

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:37.340
So the question is,
why do you choose A2?

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:41.030
So you're going to choose
whatever your policy decides.

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:42.900
So in a policy when
you're starting out,

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:45.030
you don't which
is higher reward.

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:48.060
So you're just going to maybe
choose something random.

00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:51.170
So if you happen
to choose A2 then

00:18:51.170 --> 00:18:53.430
you might upweight
that and get reward 9.

00:18:53.430 --> 00:18:57.120
I mean, it's actually worse
because you say wow, reward 9.

00:18:57.120 --> 00:18:57.990
This is great.

00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:00.570
And you-- the more
you choose it,

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:02.250
the more you're
updating against it.

00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:04.550
And then you get stuck
in this local optimum

00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:06.990
where you can't even choose.

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:10.490
There's no probability of
mass left on A1 because you've

00:19:10.490 --> 00:19:12.145
updated against A2 so much.

00:19:16.610 --> 00:19:20.660
So here's the key
idea behind baselines

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:23.750
is that you're just going to
maximize the baseline reward.

00:19:23.750 --> 00:19:25.610
Instead of expected
reward, we're

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:29.120
just going to subtract
off some B of S

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:31.110
called the baseline function.

00:19:31.110 --> 00:19:37.230
So this is just the
expected reward shifted down

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:42.780
by a constant expectation of
B of S. And the beauty of this

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:47.890
is that this quantity doesn't
depend on the policy pi.

00:19:47.890 --> 00:19:49.680
So even if you
expand it out, you'll

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:54.510
have integral over P
of S times pi a given S

00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:57.730
but integral over a
given S is just one.

00:19:57.730 --> 00:19:59.100
So this is just--

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:03.160
it doesn't depend on
the policy at all.

00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:07.710
And so of course
optimizing expectation R

00:20:07.710 --> 00:20:13.470
is identical to optimizing
expectation of R minus this

00:20:13.470 --> 00:20:16.410
because it's just a constant.

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:19.090
So that's the whole
idea of baselining.

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:23.460
It preserves the
optimization problem.

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:28.890
And now, if you do naive policy
gradient on this new objective

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:33.940
then basically you
sample a S, you sample A,

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:39.220
and then you're just
looking at this difference.

00:20:39.220 --> 00:20:46.910
So notice that this is true,
this works for any p of S.

00:20:46.910 --> 00:20:49.528
As long as P of S
doesn't depend on a which

00:20:49.528 --> 00:20:50.570
it's not in the argument.

00:20:50.570 --> 00:20:54.640
So it can't depend A,
then this is valid.

00:20:54.640 --> 00:20:58.240
So now, the question is, well
over all possible functions p

00:20:58.240 --> 00:21:00.850
of S, which one should we use?

00:21:00.850 --> 00:21:04.360
And let's just revisit
this two state example just

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:06.580
to provide some intuition.

00:21:06.580 --> 00:21:11.720
I'm going to make
some assumptions.

00:21:11.720 --> 00:21:14.260
You assume your initial policy
is a uniform distribution

00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:18.520
over all S and A, and your
gradient of your policy

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:22.870
initially is one just
to ignore that for now.

00:21:22.870 --> 00:21:30.510
So if you look at the
variance over your gradients,

00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:34.270
you'll see that some of the time
you get 11, sometimes you get 9,

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:36.370
and sometimes you get 0,
and sometimes you get 2,

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:39.510
and the variance is 5.3.

00:21:39.510 --> 00:21:48.490
So if you define a baseline,
which is 10 for S1 and 1 for S2,

00:21:48.490 --> 00:21:50.320
then let's see what happens.

00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:54.880
So you subtract off the baseline
from these reward values.

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:56.800
So for S1 you subtract 10.

00:21:56.800 --> 00:21:59.700
For S2 you subtract 1.

00:21:59.700 --> 00:22:04.390
So now, the baseline
variance is a lot less.

00:22:04.390 --> 00:22:05.860
It's only 1.1.

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:08.760
So we reduce the
variance from 5 to 1

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:13.270
which is pretty good by just
subtracting a baseline, which,

00:22:13.270 --> 00:22:17.606
again, doesn't affect your
end results in expectation.

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:23.910
So I think there
is the assumption

00:22:23.910 --> 00:22:29.260
that this expectation of P of S
doesn't depend on the policy pi.

00:22:29.260 --> 00:22:31.570
So I was wondering
if it is OK still

00:22:31.570 --> 00:22:35.680
for us to initialize like B as
let's say, the initial the very

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:38.108
original frozen policy.

00:22:38.108 --> 00:22:38.900
So a good question.

00:22:38.900 --> 00:22:41.780
So B of S is not supposed
to depend on policy.

00:22:41.780 --> 00:22:45.370
But your question is, can I
just take an initial policy

00:22:45.370 --> 00:22:46.940
and set B of S to S?

00:22:46.940 --> 00:22:47.780
So absolutely.

00:22:47.780 --> 00:22:50.530
So it's one of these things
with reinforcement learning

00:22:50.530 --> 00:22:53.170
you have to be a bit careful
with is what's constant

00:22:53.170 --> 00:22:54.170
and what's not.

00:22:54.170 --> 00:22:59.090
So you can always take a frozen
model and say that's a constant.

00:22:59.090 --> 00:23:01.940
And as long as your
expression doesn't

00:23:01.940 --> 00:23:04.190
depend on that, that's
actually treated as a constant,

00:23:04.190 --> 00:23:07.160
you're not differentiating
through it, then it's all fine.

00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:11.950
Which means also that
if you are sampling

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:14.380
and you're performing
the update,

00:23:14.380 --> 00:23:17.810
that you have to keep an
old copy of that policy.

00:23:17.810 --> 00:23:20.870
Because of hope, if that
thing is changing, now,

00:23:20.870 --> 00:23:22.660
you can get into
these weird settings

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:26.940
where you're not actually-- it's
not actually a constant anymore.

00:23:26.940 --> 00:23:28.740
And then I will
follow up and be like,

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:34.050
what if we visualize a pi as
the policy from the last step,

00:23:34.050 --> 00:23:35.630
the last radius?

00:23:35.630 --> 00:23:37.700
Instead of the
very, very original.

00:23:37.700 --> 00:23:38.970
So you can do this.

00:23:38.970 --> 00:23:45.090
So typically, what you do is
you essentially have a policy.

00:23:45.090 --> 00:23:50.130
It's an old policy or
anchor for a while.

00:23:50.130 --> 00:23:52.610
Or if you're doing PPO
you have a value function,

00:23:52.610 --> 00:23:56.360
and then you hold it for a while
and then you do some updates

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:59.190
and then you update it on.

00:23:59.190 --> 00:24:04.190
So I mean, in practice
is what you do.

00:24:04.190 --> 00:24:06.980
Part of this, it
can't depend on,

00:24:06.980 --> 00:24:12.020
is really like for the
theory to work out.

00:24:12.020 --> 00:24:17.545
But in practice there's
all things that people do.

00:24:20.330 --> 00:24:24.750
So what we showed is that
at least for this example,

00:24:24.750 --> 00:24:27.150
anecdotally you can
define some baseline

00:24:27.150 --> 00:24:29.500
that reduces the variance.

00:24:29.500 --> 00:24:31.770
So this is giving you
hopefully, a concrete sense

00:24:31.770 --> 00:24:33.820
of the power of a baseline.

00:24:33.820 --> 00:24:38.490
In the lower variance you have
the faster the convergence.

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:43.600
So you might wonder what is
the optimal baseline to use.

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:46.500
And if you do some
math, you realize

00:24:46.500 --> 00:24:51.570
there's actually a very nice
closed form solution for this.

00:24:51.570 --> 00:24:53.650
This is written for
one parameter models.

00:24:53.650 --> 00:24:58.560
If it's multi-dimensional I
think you have some covariances.

00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:04.200
And this is just expectation of
this derivative squared times

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:09.930
R divided normalized by the
same expression without the R.

00:25:09.930 --> 00:25:15.900
So this is in general
annoying to compute especially

00:25:15.900 --> 00:25:18.540
if you have high dimensions.

00:25:18.540 --> 00:25:24.650
And because this thing is going
to be some of you covariance.

00:25:24.650 --> 00:25:31.010
So typically, what people do
is you just ignore this stuff

00:25:31.010 --> 00:25:33.830
and you say, well, I'm
just going to try--

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:40.980
I'm going to set baseline equals
expected reward divided given S.

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:43.020
So this is not optimal.

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:46.110
But it's a good heuristic.

00:25:46.110 --> 00:25:48.020
And even this is--

00:25:48.020 --> 00:25:49.290
you can't compute this.

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:51.480
Because anything with
expectation you can't compute.

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:52.740
You can only estimate.

00:25:52.740 --> 00:25:57.140
But this is now I think a good
guiding principle for what

00:25:57.140 --> 00:25:59.900
you want your baseline to be.

00:25:59.900 --> 00:26:04.340
It's the expected
reward given your state.

00:26:04.340 --> 00:26:10.550
So just to recap, we said we
have the naive policy gradient.

00:26:10.550 --> 00:26:15.300
We can introduce this baseline
as valid for any B of S.

00:26:15.300 --> 00:26:17.540
And now, we've come
to a conclusion

00:26:17.540 --> 00:26:19.910
that maybe this is
a good choice if we

00:26:19.910 --> 00:26:26.280
can try to get a handle on
expected reward given S.

00:26:26.280 --> 00:26:30.090
So this particular
choice has connections

00:26:30.090 --> 00:26:31.300
to advantage functions.

00:26:31.300 --> 00:26:35.670
So in reinforcement
learning, you typically

00:26:35.670 --> 00:26:39.810
have a value function, which
is the expected reward given

00:26:39.810 --> 00:26:41.340
a state.

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:43.350
And then you have a
Q function, which is

00:26:43.350 --> 00:26:46.000
expected reward given a state.

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:48.900
And I've taken a
particular action.

00:26:48.900 --> 00:26:50.970
And note here that
Q and R are the same

00:26:50.970 --> 00:26:54.810
because we assume
outcome rewards and a has

00:26:54.810 --> 00:26:55.600
all the actions.

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:58.020
In general, R is going
to be like the returns

00:26:58.020 --> 00:27:01.420
from that state rather
than just a single reward.

00:27:01.420 --> 00:27:03.660
Those two are same for now.

00:27:03.660 --> 00:27:05.640
And the advantage
function is just

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:09.810
defined as the difference
between the reward I would

00:27:09.810 --> 00:27:11.970
get if I took action and--

00:27:11.970 --> 00:27:18.456
sorry, if I average over
possible actions in.

00:27:18.456 --> 00:27:20.750
In particular, it's asking--

00:27:20.750 --> 00:27:25.510
it's measuring how much better
is action A than the expected

00:27:25.510 --> 00:27:28.450
value that I get from S. What
is the advantage of taking

00:27:28.450 --> 00:27:33.370
A as opposed to just going
along with the baseline--

00:27:33.370 --> 00:27:35.290
sorry, baseline is ambiguous.

00:27:35.290 --> 00:27:38.980
But we're going with the policy.

00:27:38.980 --> 00:27:47.170
So then if you look at B
equals this heuristic form

00:27:47.170 --> 00:27:54.400
that we talked about earlier,
then the baseline reward where

00:27:54.400 --> 00:27:58.900
you just subtract off mu of S
is identical to the advantage.

00:27:58.900 --> 00:28:01.930
So you can interpret
the baseline

00:28:01.930 --> 00:28:05.890
if you have the
advantage-- sorry,

00:28:05.890 --> 00:28:09.700
if you have this
choice of baseline

00:28:09.700 --> 00:28:15.020
is identical to
optimizing the vantage.

00:28:15.020 --> 00:28:19.730
So that's just to give you
some intuition behind what

00:28:19.730 --> 00:28:23.720
this baseline is doing.

00:28:23.720 --> 00:28:27.140
In general, we
are probably going

00:28:27.140 --> 00:28:30.080
to deviate from that because
we can't estimate any of-- we

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:32.270
can't compute these
expectations exactly, so we're

00:28:32.270 --> 00:28:34.700
going to do something else.

00:28:34.700 --> 00:28:39.980
So in particular, I'm just
going to let delta denote

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:45.510
whatever thing we put
in to the estimate.

00:28:45.510 --> 00:28:50.870
So all of the methods are
going to basically take

00:28:50.870 --> 00:28:54.950
the gradient of the log
policy and times something

00:28:54.950 --> 00:28:57.110
that's going to be
based on the reward.

00:28:57.110 --> 00:29:02.330
So all the algorithms
see, your PPO, GRPO,

00:29:02.330 --> 00:29:05.060
have this form where
you're basically you

00:29:05.060 --> 00:29:06.720
rolled out and got an action.

00:29:06.720 --> 00:29:08.750
And now, the
question is, how much

00:29:08.750 --> 00:29:10.950
do you want to move
towards that action?

00:29:10.950 --> 00:29:15.960
And it's going to
depend on the reward.

00:29:15.960 --> 00:29:18.050
So you can put the reward here.

00:29:18.050 --> 00:29:19.580
Then that's naive
policy gradient.

00:29:19.580 --> 00:29:26.800
You can do reward minus some
estimate of the expectation.

00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:29.780
And you can also do divide
by the standard deviation,

00:29:29.780 --> 00:29:33.820
which is what GRPO does
which we'll see later.

00:29:33.820 --> 00:29:35.930
But this is the general form.

00:29:35.930 --> 00:29:40.960
And so the exact form of
prior isn't that important

00:29:40.960 --> 00:29:45.610
because next year there's
going to be GRPO 2 or whatever.

00:29:45.610 --> 00:29:48.460
But all of this policy
gradient and stuff

00:29:48.460 --> 00:29:52.245
is I'll be giving the
same lecture next year.

00:29:57.310 --> 00:30:00.430
Speaking of lecture, you can
check out Chelsea's deep RL

00:30:00.430 --> 00:30:05.860
class for a few more
derivations and intuitions about

00:30:05.860 --> 00:30:06.960
RL in general.

00:30:10.480 --> 00:30:14.050
So any questions
about policy gradient

00:30:14.050 --> 00:30:15.960
and baselines and advantages?

00:30:22.850 --> 00:30:27.530
Just for the baseline
like a baseline reward.

00:30:27.530 --> 00:30:29.090
Is that what baseline?

00:30:29.090 --> 00:30:31.230
So what's the
definition of a base--

00:30:31.230 --> 00:30:35.840
a baseline is any function
that B of S which only depends

00:30:35.840 --> 00:30:40.010
on S and A. That
you're essentially

00:30:40.010 --> 00:30:45.230
sticking into a policy
gradient algorithm.

00:30:45.230 --> 00:30:47.520
You can think about it that way.

00:30:47.520 --> 00:30:49.820
So basically, you
update policy gradient

00:30:49.820 --> 00:30:51.180
will update based on this.

00:30:51.180 --> 00:30:53.620
And baseline is whatever
function you choose there.

00:30:58.040 --> 00:31:00.870
So those of you who have
seen control variants,

00:31:00.870 --> 00:31:04.016
this is basically the
same idea from statistics.

00:31:06.890 --> 00:31:11.550
So now, let's try to
dig a little bit deeper.

00:31:11.550 --> 00:31:16.390
We're going to actually define
a simple model, a simple task,

00:31:16.390 --> 00:31:20.250
and actually walk through
some code that may or may not

00:31:20.250 --> 00:31:22.690
look like assignment five.

00:31:22.690 --> 00:31:27.210
And we'll try to
hopefully do some runs.

00:31:27.210 --> 00:31:33.430
So a lot of this exposition is
based on the GRPO algorithm.

00:31:33.430 --> 00:31:36.090
So this slide I might
have to update next year.

00:31:36.090 --> 00:31:40.110
But it's trying to generalize
it beyond the particular choices

00:31:40.110 --> 00:31:41.700
here.

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:45.420
So GRPO remember what
Tatsu said last time,

00:31:45.420 --> 00:31:49.920
it interesting lineage
where you had PPO

00:31:49.920 --> 00:31:57.990
and then PPO and GRPO is
simplification of PPO as opposed

00:31:57.990 --> 00:32:00.040
to algorithms.

00:32:00.040 --> 00:32:03.180
You start simple and
then grow in complexity.

00:32:03.180 --> 00:32:10.750
But really, I think GRPO exists
in 2024 as opposed to in 2000--

00:32:10.750 --> 00:32:14.780
I think it was '17 or something
when PPO was introduced

00:32:14.780 --> 00:32:17.140
is, because the
language model setting

00:32:17.140 --> 00:32:21.770
provides some natural
structure that motivates GRPO.

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:24.680
You can't really
do GRPO naturally,

00:32:24.680 --> 00:32:30.370
I think in classical RL settings
with the robot trying to walk.

00:32:30.370 --> 00:32:35.530
And the particular thing is
that in the language modeling

00:32:35.530 --> 00:32:37.720
setting, you have a prompt,
you can generate a bunch

00:32:37.720 --> 00:32:40.520
of responses from that prompt.

00:32:40.520 --> 00:32:43.060
And this gives you a
natural group structure

00:32:43.060 --> 00:32:46.390
to compute essentially
the baseline, which

00:32:46.390 --> 00:32:48.850
turns out to be the
average over the rewards.

00:32:48.850 --> 00:32:53.720
The whole point is that you're
trying to minimize the variance.

00:32:53.720 --> 00:32:57.020
And the group is a
natural comparison set.

00:32:57.020 --> 00:32:59.300
That's why this is relative.

00:32:59.300 --> 00:33:00.740
It's relative to the group.

00:33:00.740 --> 00:33:03.940
Whereas if every
rollout looks different

00:33:03.940 --> 00:33:05.690
there's no natural
group structure,

00:33:05.690 --> 00:33:08.440
then GRPO doesn't
really make sense.

00:33:08.440 --> 00:33:11.830
And you have to find something
else and that's something else,

00:33:11.830 --> 00:33:13.980
generally, tends to be
a value function, which

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:17.070
aggregates over all the
possible states and actions.

00:33:17.070 --> 00:33:21.180
And here in this case, you
have an empirical estimate

00:33:21.180 --> 00:33:24.960
over all the possible responses
without considering everything

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:26.750
because of the LM setting.

00:33:29.440 --> 00:33:31.410
So here's the pseudocode.

00:33:31.410 --> 00:33:36.610
We're going to look over it in
actual Python in just a second.

00:33:36.610 --> 00:33:39.390
But I'll just flash it up here.

00:33:39.390 --> 00:33:43.710
So let's define a simple task.

00:33:43.710 --> 00:33:46.560
So I wanted something
simple enough

00:33:46.560 --> 00:33:50.640
where I can actually train a
very simple model on my laptop

00:33:50.640 --> 00:33:51.780
without--

00:33:51.780 --> 00:33:55.210
and running a language model
transformer wasn't going to.

00:33:55.210 --> 00:33:56.980
I mean, I don't
have a fancy laptop,

00:33:56.980 --> 00:33:58.630
so that wasn't going to happen.

00:33:58.630 --> 00:34:01.510
So here's a simple
task, sorting n numbers.

00:34:01.510 --> 00:34:06.240
So the prompt is n
numbers and the response

00:34:06.240 --> 00:34:08.820
is n numbers that
are hopefully sorted.

00:34:08.820 --> 00:34:11.260
So now, you have
to define a reward

00:34:11.260 --> 00:34:13.699
to complete the definition
of the environment.

00:34:13.699 --> 00:34:18.400
And the reward should capture
how close to the sorted ground

00:34:18.400 --> 00:34:20.739
truth the response is.

00:34:20.739 --> 00:34:25.760
So immediately, you actually see
one of the interesting things

00:34:25.760 --> 00:34:27.760
in reinforcement learning
is that you can define

00:34:27.760 --> 00:34:29.870
a reward in different ways.

00:34:29.870 --> 00:34:32.560
So for example, you
can define a reward

00:34:32.560 --> 00:34:37.870
which is one, if it's sorted and
0 otherwise, which is presumably

00:34:37.870 --> 00:34:38.719
what you care about.

00:34:38.719 --> 00:34:41.517
But you're going to remember
the sparse reward problem.

00:34:41.517 --> 00:34:43.850
If you start out with a policy
that doesn't do anything,

00:34:43.850 --> 00:34:47.360
you're mostly going
to get 0 reward,

00:34:47.360 --> 00:34:49.940
and you're not going to
really make much progress.

00:34:49.940 --> 00:34:53.116
So let's define a reward
that gives partial credit.

00:34:53.116 --> 00:34:54.699
So we're going to
define a reward that

00:34:54.699 --> 00:34:57.670
returns the number of positions
where the response matches

00:34:57.670 --> 00:34:59.270
the ground truth.

00:34:59.270 --> 00:35:04.550
So you-- basically, the
code looks like this.

00:35:04.550 --> 00:35:08.240
You look at the ground
truth, which is the prompt.

00:35:08.240 --> 00:35:12.423
And you just count the number
of positions where the response

00:35:12.423 --> 00:35:13.465
matches the ground truth.

00:35:16.170 --> 00:35:19.520
So the reward for
the correct answer

00:35:19.520 --> 00:35:23.090
is 4 because 4 positions match.

00:35:23.090 --> 00:35:28.820
If you have this, thing that
doesn't really solve the task,

00:35:28.820 --> 00:35:29.880
then reward is one.

00:35:29.880 --> 00:35:33.410
Because I think you got
lucky and you put a 2

00:35:33.410 --> 00:35:35.480
in the second position.

00:35:35.480 --> 00:35:42.750
And you have this thing which
also is not solving the problem.

00:35:42.750 --> 00:35:47.990
But you look at this and say,
well, it's really trying.

00:35:47.990 --> 00:35:53.720
And again, you get a 1 because
the 0 is in the right position.

00:35:53.720 --> 00:35:56.840
So that's one reward
you could use.

00:35:56.840 --> 00:36:01.380
Maybe you're unhappy with this
because this is pretty far off.

00:36:01.380 --> 00:36:02.460
And this is closer.

00:36:02.460 --> 00:36:05.180
But yet they're all
getting reward one.

00:36:05.180 --> 00:36:07.000
So let's define an
alternative reward

00:36:07.000 --> 00:36:08.900
that gives more partial credit.

00:36:08.900 --> 00:36:11.650
So what I'm going
to do here is I'm

00:36:11.650 --> 00:36:17.140
going to give one
point for each token

00:36:17.140 --> 00:36:19.990
in the prompt that shows
up in the response.

00:36:19.990 --> 00:36:26.320
So at least you output the
tokens that are in the input.

00:36:26.320 --> 00:36:27.610
Not too much to ask.

00:36:27.610 --> 00:36:29.980
So for this
particular example, I

00:36:29.980 --> 00:36:32.210
get an inclusion reward of four.

00:36:32.210 --> 00:36:36.160
And then I'm going to get one
point for each adjacent pair

00:36:36.160 --> 00:36:38.463
in the response that's sorted.

00:36:41.740 --> 00:36:45.560
So that's a 3, because there's
3 pairs of adjacent pairs.

00:36:45.560 --> 00:36:47.420
And the reward is the sum.

00:36:47.420 --> 00:36:49.150
So that's 7.

00:36:49.150 --> 00:36:53.530
So if you're paying attention
you realize that this reward is

00:36:53.530 --> 00:36:56.180
actually has a loophole in it.

00:36:56.180 --> 00:36:59.050
But I'll let you
figure that one out.

00:36:59.050 --> 00:37:04.160
So now, this particular
very wrong answer,

00:37:04.160 --> 00:37:07.250
it gets a reward of 3 and
this one gets a reward of six

00:37:07.250 --> 00:37:10.110
because it's really
trying its best.

00:37:10.110 --> 00:37:15.650
And we give a lot of partial
credit for RL algorithms.

00:37:15.650 --> 00:37:17.940
So that's the task.

00:37:17.940 --> 00:37:20.870
And in general, we're going
to go with the second reward

00:37:20.870 --> 00:37:21.480
function.

00:37:21.480 --> 00:37:23.130
So the task is clear.

00:37:23.130 --> 00:37:24.650
That's an environment.

00:37:24.650 --> 00:37:28.260
So now, let's define a
model to solve this task.

00:37:28.260 --> 00:37:31.970
And so we're going to define
a really simple model.

00:37:31.970 --> 00:37:35.000
We're going to assume the
prompt and the response length

00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:38.900
are fixed and of
the same length.

00:37:38.900 --> 00:37:43.460
When I was trying to iterate
on examples, there was one--

00:37:43.460 --> 00:37:46.190
I was doing some
of dynamic thing

00:37:46.190 --> 00:37:48.650
and it was just
like annoying to do.

00:37:48.650 --> 00:37:51.337
So I simplified it.

00:37:51.337 --> 00:37:53.420
I'm going to try to capture
positional information

00:37:53.420 --> 00:37:55.590
with separate
position parameters,

00:37:55.590 --> 00:37:59.360
because obviously, position
is the name of the game here.

00:37:59.360 --> 00:38:03.420
Or position matters
for sorting obviously.

00:38:03.420 --> 00:38:07.290
And one thing I'm going to
do is decode each position

00:38:07.290 --> 00:38:08.800
in the response independently.

00:38:08.800 --> 00:38:10.630
So this is not a
regressive model.

00:38:10.630 --> 00:38:12.300
I'm just going to
encode the sequence

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:15.370
and I'm just going to output
all the tokens separately.

00:38:15.370 --> 00:38:20.140
Not what you want to do
generally in real life,

00:38:20.140 --> 00:38:22.800
except for I guess in
some speculative decoding

00:38:22.800 --> 00:38:26.550
it can be helpful to do
this in limited doses.

00:38:26.550 --> 00:38:29.100
But it makes the
code a lot cleaner

00:38:29.100 --> 00:38:31.323
because this
autoregressive generation.

00:38:31.323 --> 00:38:33.490
Now, that's the part which
was getting really hairy.

00:38:33.490 --> 00:38:35.830
So despite this simplify it.

00:38:35.830 --> 00:38:37.830
So here's the model.

00:38:37.830 --> 00:38:42.570
So I have the embedding
matrix, I have--

00:38:42.570 --> 00:38:44.610
for each position,
I have a matrix

00:38:44.610 --> 00:38:52.710
for basically transforming for
decoding and a matrix for--

00:38:52.710 --> 00:38:55.230
sorry encoding
and then decoding.

00:38:55.230 --> 00:39:00.670
So that'll be easier to explain
as I look at the forward pass.

00:39:00.670 --> 00:39:02.860
So I start with a prompt.

00:39:02.860 --> 00:39:04.810
I'm going to use this
notation in general

00:39:04.810 --> 00:39:09.100
to denote the dimensions
of the tensor here.

00:39:09.100 --> 00:39:12.280
So there's a batch dimension,
which is the number of examples

00:39:12.280 --> 00:39:13.700
and the number of prompts.

00:39:13.700 --> 00:39:16.150
And then a position
dimension, which is obviously

00:39:16.150 --> 00:39:18.310
the position I'm in.

00:39:18.310 --> 00:39:23.090
So I'm going to
generate responses.

00:39:23.090 --> 00:39:26.290
So this is inference.

00:39:26.290 --> 00:39:29.290
So let's walk through this.

00:39:29.290 --> 00:39:31.610
So just some terminology.

00:39:31.610 --> 00:39:35.780
So I have a batch size which
is the number of prompts here.

00:39:35.780 --> 00:39:38.680
Each prompt has a
particular length 3.

00:39:38.680 --> 00:39:40.720
Number of responses
per prompt I'm

00:39:40.720 --> 00:39:43.310
going to generate
in this case 2.

00:39:43.310 --> 00:39:45.950
And the response length is 4.

00:39:45.950 --> 00:39:49.150
In the sorting task
this response length

00:39:49.150 --> 00:39:53.890
is going to be identical
to the prompt length.

00:39:53.890 --> 00:40:01.560
So first I'm going to pass
the prompts through the model

00:40:01.560 --> 00:40:04.590
to get logits, which
is going to give

00:40:04.590 --> 00:40:07.740
me for each prompt
and each position

00:40:07.740 --> 00:40:10.590
a distribution over tokens.

00:40:10.590 --> 00:40:12.210
So what this looks
like is I'm going

00:40:12.210 --> 00:40:16.980
to embed the prompts
into this dimensionality.

00:40:16.980 --> 00:40:22.890
And now, I'm going to transform
using the per prompt position

00:40:22.890 --> 00:40:23.702
matrix.

00:40:23.702 --> 00:40:24.910
And I'm going to collapse it.

00:40:24.910 --> 00:40:32.380
So using an enzyme I have
batch position dimension 1.

00:40:32.380 --> 00:40:36.060
And then this weight
is per every position

00:40:36.060 --> 00:40:39.070
I have this dim 1 to
dim 2 transformation.

00:40:39.070 --> 00:40:42.540
And I'm going to end
up with batch dim 2.

00:40:42.540 --> 00:40:47.640
So I've summed out the
position and dim 1.

00:40:47.640 --> 00:40:50.910
And then given that
vector for each prompt,

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:55.230
I'm going to for each
position apply this matrix

00:40:55.230 --> 00:40:57.850
and get back a vector.

00:40:57.850 --> 00:41:03.160
And then finally, given a vector
for each position in prompt,

00:41:03.160 --> 00:41:08.500
I'm going to convert that into
a logits over my vocabulary.

00:41:08.500 --> 00:41:14.210
So pretty-- I mean it's
a simple model I made up.

00:41:14.210 --> 00:41:17.890
I don't know if it is
actually any useful--

00:41:17.890 --> 00:41:18.680
anything useful.

00:41:18.680 --> 00:41:23.140
But it fit this did
this toy example.

00:41:23.140 --> 00:41:26.170
So now I have logits.

00:41:26.170 --> 00:41:29.140
And this is just shenanigans
to deal with the fact

00:41:29.140 --> 00:41:32.360
that torch multinomial
needs something flattened.

00:41:32.360 --> 00:41:36.940
So I'm going to essentially
basically flatten this

00:41:36.940 --> 00:41:39.860
into a batch times
position by vocab.

00:41:39.860 --> 00:41:43.700
I'm going to sample responses.

00:41:43.700 --> 00:41:47.710
And that's going to
give me batch position

00:41:47.710 --> 00:41:50.920
number of responses
which I'm going to call

00:41:50.920 --> 00:41:52.550
trial is that dimension.

00:41:52.550 --> 00:41:56.380
And I'm going to
basically rearrange

00:41:56.380 --> 00:41:59.450
things to make it so that
it's for every prompt,

00:41:59.450 --> 00:42:02.860
for every trial, I'm going to
get a sequence of examples.

00:42:06.420 --> 00:42:11.640
So maybe I'll pause just to
check that everyone's with me.

00:42:11.640 --> 00:42:13.315
So this is nice.

00:42:16.220 --> 00:42:18.170
This is usually where
you would call out

00:42:18.170 --> 00:42:20.840
to VLM and do the inference.

00:42:20.840 --> 00:42:24.120
I'm just doing this here because
it's a very simple model.

00:42:24.120 --> 00:42:28.107
And I can just everything is
just like matrix operations.

00:42:28.107 --> 00:42:29.940
The responses are going
to be very close by.

00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:34.500
We are sampling from
the same logits.

00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:36.560
And responses are
going to be close by.

00:42:36.560 --> 00:42:39.780
The responses are
going to be close by.

00:42:39.780 --> 00:42:43.190
So we're sampling
responses independently

00:42:43.190 --> 00:42:44.850
from that distribution.

00:42:44.850 --> 00:42:47.820
So they're definitely
going to be similar.

00:42:47.820 --> 00:42:50.690
But that would be the same as if
you sample from a language model

00:42:50.690 --> 00:42:51.797
as well.

00:42:51.797 --> 00:42:54.380
The only difference between a
language model is autoregressive

00:42:54.380 --> 00:42:56.600
and this is independent.

00:42:56.600 --> 00:42:57.100
Trust me.

00:42:57.100 --> 00:43:01.220
The point was, shall we change
the temperature, basically?

00:43:01.220 --> 00:43:01.720
Yeah.

00:43:01.720 --> 00:43:03.220
So you could change
the temperature.

00:43:03.220 --> 00:43:05.020
You can do various things.

00:43:05.020 --> 00:43:11.310
I'm trying to be faithful
to the algorithm.

00:43:11.310 --> 00:43:13.660
So I get my responses.

00:43:13.660 --> 00:43:17.560
I can compute a reward
over all these responses.

00:43:17.560 --> 00:43:21.790
And this basically,
goes through.

00:43:21.790 --> 00:43:24.990
And for every prompt
and every response

00:43:24.990 --> 00:43:26.560
I just call this
reward function,

00:43:26.560 --> 00:43:28.680
which we looked at earlier.

00:43:28.680 --> 00:43:31.350
Nothing too interesting there.

00:43:31.350 --> 00:43:37.260
And-- sorry, this is
a little bit long.

00:43:37.260 --> 00:43:38.700
Oh, no.

00:43:38.700 --> 00:43:40.560
Well this part is
going to be far less

00:43:40.560 --> 00:43:43.770
interesting because I thought
I fixed the random seed.

00:43:43.770 --> 00:43:46.980
Generally, you wanted a
little bit more variance

00:43:46.980 --> 00:43:49.650
in the rewards.

00:43:49.650 --> 00:43:53.130
Anyway, so the rewards
all happen to be 3.

00:43:53.130 --> 00:43:56.750
So this is going to make
some of this other stuff

00:43:56.750 --> 00:44:00.200
less interesting,
but nonetheless, I'll

00:44:00.200 --> 00:44:01.690
go through the computation here.

00:44:04.640 --> 00:44:09.960
So there's this function
called compute deltas,

00:44:09.960 --> 00:44:13.820
which takes the rewards and
converts them into the delta

00:44:13.820 --> 00:44:15.870
that I'm going to
use for my updates.

00:44:15.870 --> 00:44:19.440
And the framework is
there's many ways--

00:44:19.440 --> 00:44:22.490
many choices here,
some of which you will

00:44:22.490 --> 00:44:26.270
explore in your assignment.

00:44:26.270 --> 00:44:32.810
So for example, if you can just
return delta equals the rewards,

00:44:32.810 --> 00:44:38.400
you can center the rewards.

00:44:38.400 --> 00:44:43.580
So you can compute the mean
over all of the responses

00:44:43.580 --> 00:44:45.300
for each prompt.

00:44:45.300 --> 00:44:47.250
So this is what this is doing.

00:44:47.250 --> 00:44:49.670
And then you can just
subtract off the mean

00:44:49.670 --> 00:44:51.600
that gives you center rewards.

00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:54.480
And to answer a question
from earlier, this

00:44:54.480 --> 00:44:58.630
is where if your rewards
look 1 and a bunch of zeros,

00:44:58.630 --> 00:45:01.110
your average is going to
be like point, let's say

00:45:01.110 --> 00:45:05.370
0.1, which means that if you
subtract that, now, you're

00:45:05.370 --> 00:45:08.700
going to end up with a bunch of
minus 1's and something that's

00:45:08.700 --> 00:45:10.210
slightly below 1.

00:45:10.210 --> 00:45:12.300
And therefore, you're
going to perform

00:45:12.300 --> 00:45:16.710
updates on all those
negative examples as well.

00:45:16.710 --> 00:45:18.910
So that's central rewards.

00:45:18.910 --> 00:45:24.700
And then you can do this thing
in GRPO where you normalize.

00:45:24.700 --> 00:45:27.060
So you center the
rewards, and then you

00:45:27.060 --> 00:45:31.240
divide by the standard deviation
and add something small.

00:45:31.240 --> 00:45:34.170
So you don't divide
by 0 just in case.

00:45:34.170 --> 00:45:37.890
And you return that.

00:45:37.890 --> 00:45:43.800
So one thing to notice
is that what happens

00:45:43.800 --> 00:45:45.750
if all your rewards are 5?

00:45:45.750 --> 00:45:47.580
For example.

00:45:47.580 --> 00:45:50.470
So rewards is just
going to be 5.

00:45:50.470 --> 00:45:52.990
So you're going to make a
bunch of updates, centered

00:45:52.990 --> 00:45:56.660
rewards is going to be all 0.

00:45:56.660 --> 00:45:59.230
So that means you're not
going to make any updates.

00:45:59.230 --> 00:46:00.790
So this is interesting.

00:46:00.790 --> 00:46:03.040
Think about if you
have an example where

00:46:03.040 --> 00:46:07.280
your policy is going generating
everything is the same reward.

00:46:07.280 --> 00:46:11.200
Then you're actually not
going to make any updates

00:46:11.200 --> 00:46:12.580
on that example.

00:46:12.580 --> 00:46:15.640
And the intuition,
it might or may not

00:46:15.640 --> 00:46:18.020
make sense depending on
what you're trying to do.

00:46:18.020 --> 00:46:22.150
But the intuition is
that there's no gradient.

00:46:22.150 --> 00:46:24.880
You're not-- there's-- in terms
of relative comparison between

00:46:24.880 --> 00:46:29.620
that group, there's no reason
to favor any particular example

00:46:29.620 --> 00:46:31.190
more than another one.

00:46:31.190 --> 00:46:33.340
So you're just going
to abstain and hope

00:46:33.340 --> 00:46:37.150
that some other examples
give you some signal.

00:46:37.150 --> 00:46:40.820
And the normalization means
that if all your rewards are,

00:46:40.820 --> 00:46:44.440
let's say, 10 versus
all the rewards are--

00:46:44.440 --> 00:46:47.120
if you multiply
all rewards by 100,

00:46:47.120 --> 00:46:50.680
then the normalized rewards
is invariant to the scale

00:46:50.680 --> 00:46:51.480
of the reward.

00:46:51.480 --> 00:46:55.370
So I think that's the
good thing about that.

00:46:55.370 --> 00:46:59.760
Just for fun, there's another
thing which the paper didn't do,

00:46:59.760 --> 00:47:03.540
but you might think
is a cool thing to do.

00:47:03.540 --> 00:47:05.840
So you can 0 out any reward
that isn't the maximum

00:47:05.840 --> 00:47:10.820
for each batch as well.

00:47:10.820 --> 00:47:15.330
So the idea here is that often
in reinforcement learning,

00:47:15.330 --> 00:47:17.750
sometimes you really get
into these local optimum

00:47:17.750 --> 00:47:20.580
where if you assign too
much partial credit,

00:47:20.580 --> 00:47:24.920
then the policy is going
to latch on to that

00:47:24.920 --> 00:47:30.950
and go after the low hanging
fruit instead of the thing

00:47:30.950 --> 00:47:32.370
that would give you more reward.

00:47:32.370 --> 00:47:36.420
So you could try
to just say, well,

00:47:36.420 --> 00:47:39.000
if it's not getting
the maximum reward,

00:47:39.000 --> 00:47:40.830
then I just don't want that.

00:47:40.830 --> 00:47:42.160
It's an all or nothing thing.

00:47:45.050 --> 00:47:51.740
So far what we've done, we
generate a bunch of responses,

00:47:51.740 --> 00:47:52.970
we compute rewards.

00:47:52.970 --> 00:47:58.540
And these deltas which allow
me to provide the update, now,

00:47:58.540 --> 00:48:02.050
I need to look at the other
piece, which is the log

00:48:02.050 --> 00:48:04.670
probabilities of the responses.

00:48:04.670 --> 00:48:07.450
Because remember in the
gradient-- policy gradient

00:48:07.450 --> 00:48:12.550
I have delta times the
gradient of the log policy.

00:48:12.550 --> 00:48:18.400
So this part is also
fairly straightforward.

00:48:18.400 --> 00:48:23.050
I pass this through the
model, convert the logits

00:48:23.050 --> 00:48:25.480
into log probs.

00:48:25.480 --> 00:48:27.220
And then there's
some shenanigans

00:48:27.220 --> 00:48:30.580
to because the
prompt is doesn't--

00:48:30.580 --> 00:48:34.780
is only batch pause and
responses has an extra dimension

00:48:34.780 --> 00:48:35.420
in there.

00:48:35.420 --> 00:48:42.160
So I'm just going to upcast
these log probs to that higher

00:48:42.160 --> 00:48:44.780
tensor, dimensional tensor.

00:48:44.780 --> 00:48:50.580
And I'm going to index log
probs using the responses.

00:48:50.580 --> 00:48:55.490
So the responses basically
for every batch trial position

00:48:55.490 --> 00:49:01.460
specifies the index into log
probs that I need to get.

00:49:01.460 --> 00:49:04.620
So this is batch
trial pause vocab.

00:49:04.620 --> 00:49:08.700
And this is batch trial
pause with the index.

00:49:08.700 --> 00:49:12.290
And that will give
me batch trial pause.

00:49:12.290 --> 00:49:18.930
So this gives me-- so logits
that are returned by the model

00:49:18.930 --> 00:49:22.820
give me a distribution over all
the vocabs for every position

00:49:22.820 --> 00:49:24.930
and prompt.

00:49:24.930 --> 00:49:28.500
And now, I have log probs
for the particular responses.

00:49:28.500 --> 00:49:33.170
So these are log probs for these
particular responses I got.

00:49:33.170 --> 00:49:37.890
So now, we want to
finally compute the loss.

00:49:37.890 --> 00:49:41.420
And the loss is
just going to be--

00:49:41.420 --> 00:49:44.400
and here there's another
set of choices here.

00:49:44.400 --> 00:49:49.060
So I'm trying to present
this as a menu of sorts.

00:49:49.060 --> 00:49:53.530
So the most naive thing is--
this is naive policy gradient.

00:49:53.530 --> 00:49:56.040
You just take the
deltas and the log probs

00:49:56.040 --> 00:50:00.120
and you just multiply
them together in average.

00:50:00.120 --> 00:50:05.380
So this is log probs.

00:50:05.380 --> 00:50:08.770
Remember batch trial pause
and deltas is batch trial.

00:50:08.770 --> 00:50:12.240
And this is because
it's outcome reward.

00:50:12.240 --> 00:50:15.030
You only get--
there's no position

00:50:15.030 --> 00:50:17.680
dependence on the deltas.

00:50:17.680 --> 00:50:20.190
So basically, every position
gets-- the deltas get

00:50:20.190 --> 00:50:22.270
broadcasted to every position.

00:50:22.270 --> 00:50:25.410
If you had process
reward then this deltas

00:50:25.410 --> 00:50:28.650
would be per position as well.

00:50:28.650 --> 00:50:30.660
So that's the naive thing.

00:50:30.660 --> 00:50:36.300
Just to interlude
to make sure that we

00:50:36.300 --> 00:50:38.920
into this idea of
freezing parameters.

00:50:38.920 --> 00:50:42.970
So later we'll see in
GRPO, you see these ratios.

00:50:42.970 --> 00:50:46.150
This relates to
this idea that you

00:50:46.150 --> 00:50:49.780
have a probability
of action given S

00:50:49.780 --> 00:50:54.290
and you're dividing
by some other model.

00:50:54.290 --> 00:50:57.430
And it's really
important that you

00:50:57.430 --> 00:51:01.520
can remember to freeze and not
differentiate through p_old.

00:51:01.520 --> 00:51:03.320
So just to give
you a toy example.

00:51:03.320 --> 00:51:06.250
Suppose I have a single
parameter, w, and that

00:51:06.250 --> 00:51:10.570
drives both the probability.

00:51:10.570 --> 00:51:12.140
Think about this as a policy.

00:51:12.140 --> 00:51:14.860
And also there's
an old value, which

00:51:14.860 --> 00:51:17.890
in this particular iteration
is just the same thing.

00:51:17.890 --> 00:51:20.500
And now, what happens
if I compute the ratio

00:51:20.500 --> 00:51:22.490
and do a backward pass.

00:51:22.490 --> 00:51:25.840
What would the gradient be?

00:51:25.840 --> 00:51:29.540
So I'm dividing p by p old.

00:51:29.540 --> 00:51:36.920
So the ratio is going to be one,
and the gradient of one is zero.

00:51:36.920 --> 00:51:38.715
So what are the--

00:51:41.740 --> 00:51:47.730
so it's zero which is
obviously not very helpful.

00:51:47.730 --> 00:51:49.620
So the way you do
this properly, is

00:51:49.620 --> 00:51:54.390
that you have your p as
usual, and then anything you

00:51:54.390 --> 00:51:57.670
want to treat as a constant,
you put in wrap in this no grad.

00:51:57.670 --> 00:52:01.470
And then this allows you
to get the value of--

00:52:01.470 --> 00:52:03.190
but not the whole
computation graph.

00:52:03.190 --> 00:52:04.960
And you just divide by that.

00:52:04.960 --> 00:52:08.010
And now, the
gradient is not zero.

00:52:08.010 --> 00:52:11.610
So just like a be
careful when you're--

00:52:11.610 --> 00:52:15.340
because in RL as opposed to
when you're doing pre-training,

00:52:15.340 --> 00:52:18.780
there's many different
values that all

00:52:18.780 --> 00:52:24.040
depend on different either
old or not old parameters.

00:52:26.340 --> 00:52:28.770
Why don't we discount
an outcome reward

00:52:28.770 --> 00:52:30.210
when we go back to the sequence?

00:52:30.210 --> 00:52:32.610
I guess what do you
probably have a hypothesis

00:52:32.610 --> 00:52:37.200
that the last 100 tokens
matter more than the first 100?

00:52:37.200 --> 00:52:39.600
So the question is, why
don't you discount--

00:52:39.600 --> 00:52:44.290
do some of discounting when
you have outcome rewards?

00:52:44.290 --> 00:52:48.850
And maybe the last
100 tokens matter more

00:52:48.850 --> 00:52:51.460
than the first 100 tokens.

00:52:51.460 --> 00:52:55.435
I think that is
less not so clear.

00:52:55.435 --> 00:52:57.310
I mean, certainly, like
the last tokens where

00:52:57.310 --> 00:52:59.410
you're generating, literally
generating the answer

00:52:59.410 --> 00:53:00.710
is important.

00:53:00.710 --> 00:53:04.070
But I think the problem is that
in reinforcement in general,

00:53:04.070 --> 00:53:06.160
you have to do
credit assignment.

00:53:06.160 --> 00:53:09.610
There's certainly decisions
early on that matter.

00:53:09.610 --> 00:53:13.700
In fact, they might even
matter more than what you get.

00:53:13.700 --> 00:53:16.370
Because if you basically
have the right strategy,

00:53:16.370 --> 00:53:20.420
then the stuff that happens at
the end, maybe just follows out.

00:53:20.420 --> 00:53:22.493
And the hard part in
reinforcement learning

00:53:22.493 --> 00:53:24.160
is that you don't
where to do the credit

00:53:24.160 --> 00:53:26.990
assignment, especially when
you have a sparse reward.

00:53:26.990 --> 00:53:28.960
So I think in this
setting, we're

00:53:28.960 --> 00:53:33.695
just going to smear the credit
all over or the blame all

00:53:33.695 --> 00:53:34.320
over the place.

00:53:37.810 --> 00:53:43.070
So with that
interlude, so this is

00:53:43.070 --> 00:53:46.470
important for computing
the GRPO loss.

00:53:46.470 --> 00:53:51.500
So imagine we have an
old model with an old--

00:53:51.500 --> 00:53:53.310
so this is an old checkpoint.

00:53:53.310 --> 00:53:56.930
And using this old
model, we're going

00:53:56.930 --> 00:53:59.640
to compute the log
probabilities of the responses.

00:53:59.640 --> 00:54:03.150
So that gives us the
old log probabilities.

00:54:03.150 --> 00:54:08.640
And now, let me just skip
the unclipped version.

00:54:08.640 --> 00:54:12.930
So this is the GRPO loss.

00:54:12.930 --> 00:54:14.280
So let's step through it.

00:54:14.280 --> 00:54:16.910
So it's the clipped version.

00:54:16.910 --> 00:54:21.290
So you look at the
log probs and you

00:54:21.290 --> 00:54:23.360
divide by the old
log probs which

00:54:23.360 --> 00:54:27.500
remember should be a constant.

00:54:27.500 --> 00:54:30.240
Actually, I didn't
follow my own advice.

00:54:30.240 --> 00:54:33.870
I don't think I wrapped the
old log probs in with no grad.

00:54:33.870 --> 00:54:36.060
But anyway, you
know how to do that.

00:54:36.060 --> 00:54:40.110
So you look at
this ratio and then

00:54:40.110 --> 00:54:45.240
that ratio gets
multiplied by delta.

00:54:45.240 --> 00:54:50.340
And so if you can trust
with the naive thing,

00:54:50.340 --> 00:54:53.770
you're just multiplying
the log probs times delta.

00:54:53.770 --> 00:54:55.750
So this is basically
the same thing,

00:54:55.750 --> 00:54:57.860
except for I've
divided by all log

00:54:57.860 --> 00:54:59.110
products, which is a constant.

00:54:59.110 --> 00:55:02.310
So I'm just scaling
the gradient.

00:55:02.310 --> 00:55:05.140
But now, there's another
part which is I'm clipping.

00:55:05.140 --> 00:55:07.950
And the reason I compute this
ratio is in the first place

00:55:07.950 --> 00:55:13.440
is that I'm going to clip
the gradients between 1

00:55:13.440 --> 00:55:15.640
minus epsilon and
1 plus epsilon.

00:55:15.640 --> 00:55:18.240
So this is to make sure
that the ratios don't

00:55:18.240 --> 00:55:20.410
go too out of whack.

00:55:20.410 --> 00:55:24.480
And I'm going to also multiply
these clip ratios by delta.

00:55:24.480 --> 00:55:28.300
And I'm going to take the
minimum and return it.

00:55:28.300 --> 00:55:30.300
And there's a negative
sign because I'm

00:55:30.300 --> 00:55:32.540
talking about rewards I
convert that into a loss.

00:55:37.050 --> 00:55:43.380
So this is just following
the math in your GRPO paper.

00:55:43.380 --> 00:55:47.330
And in the assignment,
you'll see this explanation

00:55:47.330 --> 00:55:49.620
of why this makes sense.

00:55:49.620 --> 00:55:54.720
So if your updates are
within the clipping range,

00:55:54.720 --> 00:55:58.680
then you just get
the standard update.

00:55:58.680 --> 00:56:03.590
But if your ratio is
outside, then the magnitude

00:56:03.590 --> 00:56:07.340
of your updates are going to
be bounded by 1 minus epsilon

00:56:07.340 --> 00:56:08.455
and 1 plus epsilon.

00:56:17.000 --> 00:56:21.410
So there's also this KL
penalty, which provides

00:56:21.410 --> 00:56:24.530
additional regularization.

00:56:24.530 --> 00:56:33.780
And so just to a quick
little math exercise here.

00:56:33.780 --> 00:56:36.980
So remember the definition of
KL between two distributions.

00:56:36.980 --> 00:56:40.780
It's your sample from the first
left distribution and its log

00:56:40.780 --> 00:56:43.750
over the left or the right.

00:56:43.750 --> 00:56:49.310
Rewrite that as you Q over
P with a negative sign.

00:56:49.310 --> 00:56:55.420
And then-- so if you're
doing KL penalty,

00:56:55.420 --> 00:56:59.030
you could just
use this directly.

00:56:59.030 --> 00:57:03.670
But there's this-- and if
you want to estimate the KL,

00:57:03.670 --> 00:57:06.790
you can just take strip
out the expectation

00:57:06.790 --> 00:57:10.120
and you get an
unbiased estimate.

00:57:10.120 --> 00:57:13.540
It turns out that this
particular estimate

00:57:13.540 --> 00:57:17.500
can have lower variance
where you just do

00:57:17.500 --> 00:57:19.700
Q over P minus the log minus 1.

00:57:19.700 --> 00:57:22.300
So it looks a little
bit weird, but you can

00:57:22.300 --> 00:57:25.640
check that this is identical.

00:57:25.640 --> 00:57:31.730
Everything's equal because
expectation Q over P is just 1.

00:57:31.730 --> 00:57:33.170
So this part is just 1.

00:57:33.170 --> 00:57:35.090
And you subtract 1, which is 0.

00:57:35.090 --> 00:57:37.510
So you're just left
with this original term.

00:57:37.510 --> 00:57:42.340
So the math works out
and the inside just

00:57:42.340 --> 00:57:44.890
gives you a better
estimate of the KL

00:57:44.890 --> 00:57:47.800
than doing this naive thing.

00:57:47.800 --> 00:57:50.320
So a lot of cases,
when you're trying

00:57:50.320 --> 00:57:53.680
to find these estimates
of the expectation,

00:57:53.680 --> 00:57:57.220
you're just trying to find
an unbiased or sometimes even

00:57:57.220 --> 00:58:01.390
biased estimate that has
lower variance than just doing

00:58:01.390 --> 00:58:02.420
the naive thing.

00:58:02.420 --> 00:58:05.890
So you try to rewrite
the inside to--

00:58:05.890 --> 00:58:09.220
so that it reduces the variance.

00:58:09.220 --> 00:58:13.430
So just a quick implementation
of the KL penalty.

00:58:13.430 --> 00:58:16.043
This is pretty straightforward.

00:58:16.043 --> 00:58:17.710
The only thing you
have to pay attention

00:58:17.710 --> 00:58:19.840
to as you're summing over
the last dimension but taking

00:58:19.840 --> 00:58:20.420
a mean.

00:58:20.420 --> 00:58:23.470
So you're summing over the
vocab but you're taking a mean

00:58:23.470 --> 00:58:25.713
over batch trial in position.

00:58:30.670 --> 00:58:35.240
So just to summarize so
far the components that

00:58:35.240 --> 00:58:38.900
are needed to introduce
the actual algorithm,

00:58:38.900 --> 00:58:44.250
you generate the responses
given a particular fixed model.

00:58:44.250 --> 00:58:49.290
From the responses, you compute
the rewards and the deltas.

00:58:49.290 --> 00:58:54.410
And in the case of
GRPO and friends,

00:58:54.410 --> 00:58:56.040
these don't depend on the model.

00:58:56.040 --> 00:58:59.490
It's just a function
of the responses.

00:58:59.490 --> 00:59:04.200
And if you think about
architecting this system,

00:59:04.200 --> 00:59:05.760
sometimes the reward
is very simple.

00:59:05.760 --> 00:59:08.100
It's just like a one
liner exact match.

00:59:08.100 --> 00:59:11.780
Sometimes the reward
will involve an agent,

00:59:11.780 --> 00:59:14.760
executing an environment
and doing all crazy things.

00:59:14.760 --> 00:59:20.480
So this could be expensive
or cheap operation,

00:59:20.480 --> 00:59:23.220
but it's independent
of the actual model,

00:59:23.220 --> 00:59:26.090
unless you have LM as a
judge, which case, that's

00:59:26.090 --> 00:59:28.370
another model.

00:59:28.370 --> 00:59:34.600
Simultaneously, you can compute
the log probs of the responses,

00:59:34.600 --> 00:59:38.800
and then you compute the
loss from the log probs

00:59:38.800 --> 00:59:43.930
and the deltas that
you got here and you

00:59:43.930 --> 00:59:49.990
combine them either clip or not
clip or you normalize or you--

00:59:49.990 --> 00:59:51.080
sorry, that's up here.

00:59:51.080 --> 00:59:54.430
So you either clip or
don't clip and then

00:59:54.430 --> 00:59:57.550
you take a gradient step.

00:59:57.550 --> 01:00:00.950
So let's try to put
this all together.

01:00:00.950 --> 01:00:03.880
So this is the full
algorithm, which

01:00:03.880 --> 01:00:10.780
is essentially a implementation
of what's in here in code.

01:00:10.780 --> 01:00:13.910
So there's some boilerplate,
I'll just skip over.

01:00:13.910 --> 01:00:19.960
So I'm going to define some
trivial data set here, 3 prompts

01:00:19.960 --> 01:00:23.110
of length 3 each.

01:00:23.110 --> 01:00:24.915
And then define the model.

01:00:27.460 --> 01:00:33.050
Optimizer, and then
I have this-- there's

01:00:33.050 --> 01:00:37.320
multiple loops here and GRPO.

01:00:37.320 --> 01:00:41.300
So there's an outer epoch loop.

01:00:41.300 --> 01:00:45.470
And here, if I'm doing the--

01:00:45.470 --> 01:00:49.470
sometimes if I'm using
the doing a KL penalty,

01:00:49.470 --> 01:00:52.670
then I'm going to
freeze the model

01:00:52.670 --> 01:00:58.700
and have that as a reference so
I can regularize towards that.

01:00:58.700 --> 01:01:02.640
And then otherwise I
sample the responses.

01:01:02.640 --> 01:01:04.080
So we talked about this.

01:01:04.080 --> 01:01:05.610
We compute the rewards.

01:01:05.610 --> 01:01:12.050
We compute the deltas depending
on what variant flavor you like.

01:01:12.050 --> 01:01:17.510
And if you're doing
the KL penalty then

01:01:17.510 --> 01:01:23.990
you also need to compute the log
probs under the reference model.

01:01:23.990 --> 01:01:31.500
And if you're doing not-- if
you're doing some of clipping,

01:01:31.500 --> 01:01:36.090
then you need to
compute also the log

01:01:36.090 --> 01:01:42.690
probs under the current model.

01:01:42.690 --> 01:01:46.450
And then you take a number
of steps given the responses.

01:01:46.450 --> 01:01:51.070
So the idea here is
that often response.

01:01:51.070 --> 01:01:52.930
Remember inference is expensive.

01:01:52.930 --> 01:01:56.100
You generate and you want to
do a bunch of gradient steps

01:01:56.100 --> 01:01:58.140
with respect to the responses.

01:01:58.140 --> 01:02:01.080
So this inner loop
is basically taking

01:02:01.080 --> 01:02:04.600
a bunch of steps with respect
to the same set of responses.

01:02:04.600 --> 01:02:07.180
And to do that, you
compute the log probs.

01:02:07.180 --> 01:02:09.480
You compute the loss.

01:02:09.480 --> 01:02:13.240
If you want, you can add a kl
penalty, which sometimes helps,

01:02:13.240 --> 01:02:15.990
sometimes doesn't really
make a difference.

01:02:15.990 --> 01:02:20.396
And then you just optimize.

01:02:26.630 --> 01:02:32.050
So that is-- maybe
I'll stop and answer

01:02:32.050 --> 01:02:35.520
any questions people have
about the setup here.

01:02:38.500 --> 01:02:44.860
So the big picture is that you
have the outer iteration, which

01:02:44.860 --> 01:02:48.640
you ignoring the kl penalty
because there's another wrinkle

01:02:48.640 --> 01:02:49.760
I'll mention later.

01:02:49.760 --> 01:02:52.730
So you generate a
bunch of responses.

01:02:52.730 --> 01:02:56.188
And then you quickly do a
bunch of iterations on them.

01:02:56.188 --> 01:02:57.730
And you generate a
bunch of responses

01:02:57.730 --> 01:02:58.970
and do some iterations.

01:02:58.970 --> 01:03:02.440
That's the basic idea.

01:03:02.440 --> 01:03:05.710
Now, there's an additional
thing which if you have the kl

01:03:05.710 --> 01:03:09.880
penalty, then you actually
regularize towards a reference

01:03:09.880 --> 01:03:14.780
model, which is changing even
slower than this outside model.

01:03:14.780 --> 01:03:17.320
So the way this is
written is actually

01:03:17.320 --> 01:03:21.770
slightly different
than the GRPO code.

01:03:21.770 --> 01:03:24.710
In the code, you actually
have three loops.

01:03:24.710 --> 01:03:30.120
I only have two because I'm just
updating the reference model

01:03:30.120 --> 01:03:36.570
a little bit infrequently.

01:03:36.570 --> 01:03:39.840
And I'm also not
doing this part.

01:03:39.840 --> 01:03:42.280
So there's a few
implementation details,

01:03:42.280 --> 01:03:44.670
but hopefully, you could
get the rough shape

01:03:44.670 --> 01:03:47.700
of what this looks like.

01:03:47.700 --> 01:03:50.223
So you actually have
of three models.

01:03:50.223 --> 01:03:52.140
You have the reference
model that you're doing

01:03:52.140 --> 01:03:53.980
cl regularization against.

01:03:53.980 --> 01:03:56.040
And then you have
the old model which

01:03:56.040 --> 01:03:58.757
you're using to compute
these importance

01:03:58.757 --> 01:04:00.840
ratios between the current
model and the old model

01:04:00.840 --> 01:04:02.350
which you're also freezing.

01:04:02.350 --> 01:04:10.860
And then updating the
current model online.

01:04:10.860 --> 01:04:14.400
So one annoying thing
about the reference model

01:04:14.400 --> 01:04:17.760
is that you actually
have to store

01:04:17.760 --> 01:04:23.700
a whole copy of the model, which
can be just doubles your memory.

01:04:23.700 --> 01:04:29.590
The old model is actually a
little bit better because you

01:04:29.590 --> 01:04:35.590
just remember if in
the code, you actually

01:04:35.590 --> 01:04:37.250
don't have to copy
the old model,

01:04:37.250 --> 01:04:39.640
you can just generate
a bunch of responses

01:04:39.640 --> 01:04:43.420
and compute the log
probs on those responses

01:04:43.420 --> 01:04:45.407
and just store the
log probabilities,

01:04:45.407 --> 01:04:46.990
because those are
going to be-- you're

01:04:46.990 --> 01:04:49.840
dealing with the same
responses in an inner loop.

01:04:49.840 --> 01:04:52.173
So you don't have to store
a whole copy of the model,

01:04:52.173 --> 01:04:53.715
you just have to
store the log probs.

01:04:59.110 --> 01:05:00.220
Question?

01:05:00.220 --> 01:05:03.430
Why don't we do kl
divergence between old policy

01:05:03.430 --> 01:05:07.095
and the latest policy instead of
doing it against your reference?

01:05:07.095 --> 01:05:08.470
So the question
is, why don't you

01:05:08.470 --> 01:05:17.080
do the kl penalty against the
old instead of the reference?

01:05:17.080 --> 01:05:19.860
The way to think
about this is that--

01:05:22.490 --> 01:05:25.310
let's see.

01:05:25.310 --> 01:05:29.840
So remember how we said we
define objective function

01:05:29.840 --> 01:05:31.260
in reinforcement learning.

01:05:31.260 --> 01:05:32.910
And we optimize that.

01:05:32.910 --> 01:05:35.840
It's a little bit of a lie.

01:05:35.840 --> 01:05:38.910
Because really you just define
this objective function,

01:05:38.910 --> 01:05:40.670
so you can compute a gradient.

01:05:40.670 --> 01:05:46.940
And the gradient you compute
and you update for a while,

01:05:46.940 --> 01:05:50.730
but then your objective
function changes.

01:05:50.730 --> 01:06:00.080
So really you can
think about let's say--

01:06:00.080 --> 01:06:02.990
so this point you
fix a reference model

01:06:02.990 --> 01:06:05.150
and then you write down
this nice objective

01:06:05.150 --> 01:06:07.850
function, which is--

01:06:07.850 --> 01:06:10.680
let's see if I don't have
the full expression here,

01:06:10.680 --> 01:06:19.610
but the expected reward
with all the expected reward

01:06:19.610 --> 01:06:22.540
and then the kl penalty.

01:06:22.540 --> 01:06:25.600
So if you change that
to quickly, then--

01:06:25.600 --> 01:06:28.780
I guess the more
you change that,

01:06:28.780 --> 01:06:32.110
then the less you're
actually like optimizing

01:06:32.110 --> 01:06:35.380
that objective function.

01:06:35.380 --> 01:06:39.790
So that's I guess
maybe one reason.

01:06:39.790 --> 01:06:44.860
I mean, honestly, I
think empirically you--

01:06:44.860 --> 01:06:48.730
it's a gradient because
depending on how many steps

01:06:48.730 --> 01:06:51.070
you take here and
how often, you can

01:06:51.070 --> 01:06:58.570
make it either more like
pi old or less like pi old.

01:06:58.570 --> 01:07:01.730
I mean, you have two
mechanisms for regularization.

01:07:01.730 --> 01:07:03.980
One is the clipping thing,
which is applied to pi old,

01:07:03.980 --> 01:07:06.120
and then the KL which
is applied to pi ref.

01:07:12.800 --> 01:07:14.020
Question.

01:07:14.020 --> 01:07:16.850
For the first iteration
GRPO for the most inner loop

01:07:16.850 --> 01:07:21.020
isn't pi theta old
equal to pi theta,

01:07:21.020 --> 01:07:23.810
is it wasteful to do that?

01:07:23.810 --> 01:07:27.500
So the first iteration
pi is equal to pi theta.

01:07:27.500 --> 01:07:31.050
So you're essentially going to--

01:07:31.050 --> 01:07:34.430
well you're still
performing an update.

01:07:34.430 --> 01:07:36.210
You're just scaling by some.

01:07:36.210 --> 01:07:39.080
Remember, pi all of this from
the point of view of the update

01:07:39.080 --> 01:07:41.210
is just a constant.

01:07:41.210 --> 01:07:44.780
And I guess the only
thing that matters

01:07:44.780 --> 01:07:48.720
is that you're not going to get
clipped because the ratio is 1.

01:07:48.720 --> 01:07:52.550
So you're just going to
guarantee to make an update.

01:07:52.550 --> 01:07:56.540
So yeah, it's fine.

01:07:56.540 --> 01:08:02.060
So maybe this is just
round this out a bit.

01:08:02.060 --> 01:08:06.900
So actually-- so I
ran some experiments.

01:08:06.900 --> 01:08:10.470
So you run the same piece
of code that I presented.

01:08:10.470 --> 01:08:16.130
I'm going to do 100 epochs
number of steps for epochs 10.

01:08:16.130 --> 01:08:21.840
And we see this learning curve.

01:08:21.840 --> 01:08:27.029
So just to maybe walk
through what this is doing.

01:08:27.029 --> 01:08:31.120
So on epoch 0, step 0,
we have this prompt.

01:08:31.120 --> 01:08:34.170
The responses it generates
a bunch of stuff.

01:08:34.170 --> 01:08:36.990
You score them, you
compute the reward,

01:08:36.990 --> 01:08:39.790
and then you compute the delta.

01:08:39.790 --> 01:08:41.920
In this case, it's
identical to the reward.

01:08:41.920 --> 01:08:45.390
So I'm going to update towards
the 3's a little bit more than

01:08:45.390 --> 01:08:47.910
the 2's.

01:08:47.910 --> 01:08:53.819
And then you see this
prompt the same thing.

01:08:53.819 --> 01:08:59.399
This one-- I'm still going
to update against all these--

01:08:59.399 --> 01:09:04.529
towards these responses even
though they all have reward 3.

01:09:04.529 --> 01:09:07.003
And then let me scroll
down and see what happens.

01:09:09.540 --> 01:09:19.270
Eventually, you see that
I'm getting reward 3.

01:09:19.270 --> 01:09:22.930
But this is not looking--

01:09:22.930 --> 01:09:27.859
I mean, it's not really
sorting is as short answer.

01:09:27.859 --> 01:09:32.870
It's producing numbers which
are more or less in the prompt,

01:09:32.870 --> 01:09:35.450
but not really sorting.

01:09:35.450 --> 01:09:37.490
Let's see how it
does at the end.

01:09:37.490 --> 01:09:42.850
So it's not terribly great.

01:09:42.850 --> 01:09:44.899
I actually haven't looked
at the other examples.

01:09:44.899 --> 01:09:49.840
So it'll be exciting to
see what happens here.

01:09:49.840 --> 01:09:54.130
So here, we're using
the centered rewards

01:09:54.130 --> 01:09:57.530
and see the mean reward
is getting higher.

01:09:57.530 --> 01:10:03.160
So it's like past 3 instead
of 2, which is promising.

01:10:03.160 --> 01:10:05.480
So let's look at this run.

01:10:05.480 --> 01:10:10.580
So notice here that I
generate some examples.

01:10:10.580 --> 01:10:14.000
Reward is 2 and 3.

01:10:14.000 --> 01:10:18.650
But because I'm
centering, I'm giving--

01:10:18.650 --> 01:10:21.560
pushing them all towards
the ones with reward 3

01:10:21.560 --> 01:10:23.850
and away from the ones
that have reward 2.

01:10:23.850 --> 01:10:28.250
So this is what
centering is doing.

01:10:28.250 --> 01:10:33.890
And if they're all the same,
then centering doesn't really--

01:10:33.890 --> 01:10:35.520
is actually going to be a no op.

01:10:35.520 --> 01:10:42.495
Whereas before I was performing
updates which was silly I think.

01:10:42.495 --> 01:10:44.370
Because if you think
about it, I'm sorting 1,

01:10:44.370 --> 01:10:49.020
2, 3, and here are all the
responses are equally bad.

01:10:49.020 --> 01:10:54.470
So updating towards these don't
really maybe make as much sense.

01:10:54.470 --> 01:10:59.000
Let's see towards the
end how this does.

01:10:59.000 --> 01:11:02.870
So still not, I
guess it's not great

01:11:02.870 --> 01:11:05.510
but it's got a little
bit higher reward.

01:11:05.510 --> 01:11:07.790
I probably need to tune
this a little bit more.

01:11:07.790 --> 01:11:12.170
But at least it's
getting reward for here

01:11:12.170 --> 01:11:13.590
for getting the right numbers.

01:11:13.590 --> 01:11:16.953
And 0, 1 it's not
the most unsorted.

01:11:20.440 --> 01:11:23.060
Here-- I think it's
getting the permutation.

01:11:23.060 --> 01:11:25.960
And here, I think
it just gets stuck.

01:11:25.960 --> 01:11:28.550
And at this point, you
see all the deltas are 0.

01:11:28.550 --> 01:11:32.450
So more training isn't really
going to help on this batch.

01:11:32.450 --> 01:11:37.390
But if you regenerate, maybe
you'll get some fresh samples

01:11:37.390 --> 01:11:39.010
and be able to update.

01:11:39.010 --> 01:11:42.670
So you can see that
it's the partial reward

01:11:42.670 --> 01:11:45.260
is a double edged sword.

01:11:45.260 --> 01:11:47.870
Because I'm giving
you four points.

01:11:47.870 --> 01:11:51.080
So it's like, it's
a good reward.

01:11:51.080 --> 01:11:54.470
But I still didn't solve
the problem completely.

01:11:54.470 --> 01:11:58.550
So I think you have to
be careful with reward,

01:11:58.550 --> 01:11:59.770
additional reward.

01:11:59.770 --> 01:12:02.590
And then finally,
the standardized--

01:12:02.590 --> 01:12:06.710
I think the normalization here
didn't really do all that much.

01:12:06.710 --> 01:12:09.620
So I'm going to skip it.

01:12:09.620 --> 01:12:11.350
One thing to I think
note is that if you

01:12:11.350 --> 01:12:14.300
look at these loss curves.

01:12:14.300 --> 01:12:17.160
You think, well,
this looks good,

01:12:17.160 --> 01:12:20.870
but why is this
looks pretty bad even

01:12:20.870 --> 01:12:22.970
though the reward is going up.

01:12:22.970 --> 01:12:27.290
And this goes back
to my statement

01:12:27.290 --> 01:12:32.360
that minimizing the loss is
a little bit of a lie here.

01:12:32.360 --> 01:12:35.060
It's not like we were
minimizing one loss function

01:12:35.060 --> 01:12:38.570
that you can measure over time,
because the set of responses

01:12:38.570 --> 01:12:40.770
is changing over time.

01:12:40.770 --> 01:12:43.130
You don't have really
a reference point

01:12:43.130 --> 01:12:44.760
for the same loss.

01:12:44.760 --> 01:12:48.680
I mean, you can measure loss
on maybe some validation set.

01:12:48.680 --> 01:12:52.160
But if you only have rewards,
then you're back to only

01:12:52.160 --> 01:12:54.320
being able to rely on reward.

01:12:54.320 --> 01:12:57.050
Because even--
think about it, even

01:12:57.050 --> 01:13:06.620
if you have a bunch of
examples where you're the model

01:13:06.620 --> 01:13:08.940
is just happy generating them.

01:13:08.940 --> 01:13:12.180
It's like the loss is only
respect to what the model is

01:13:12.180 --> 01:13:12.700
generating.

01:13:12.700 --> 01:13:15.160
So it's a self circular thing.

01:13:15.160 --> 01:13:20.670
So monitoring the losses not
really such a meaningful thing

01:13:20.670 --> 01:13:22.650
to do.

01:13:22.650 --> 01:13:26.280
So maybe just to wrap up here.

01:13:26.280 --> 01:13:29.760
So reinforcement learning,
just popping back up,

01:13:29.760 --> 01:13:33.030
I think is really exciting
because it's really

01:13:33.030 --> 01:13:37.710
I think the key to making
models that actually surpass

01:13:37.710 --> 01:13:43.860
human abilities in some sense,
because labeled data is only

01:13:43.860 --> 01:13:47.520
going to get you as far as
mimicking the behavior that's

01:13:47.520 --> 01:13:50.100
presented in the labeled data.

01:13:50.100 --> 01:13:54.010
I think the model is if you can
measure it, you can optimize it.

01:13:54.010 --> 01:13:56.100
If you have a reward.

01:13:56.100 --> 01:14:00.270
I think there is optimization
as we've seen even

01:14:00.270 --> 01:14:02.040
with this sorting
examples, I mean

01:14:02.040 --> 01:14:04.900
can be challenging in itself.

01:14:04.900 --> 01:14:08.790
But there's also perhaps a
bigger problem is, how do you

01:14:08.790 --> 01:14:10.750
design rewards that
are not hackable

01:14:10.750 --> 01:14:13.010
and environments that
are generalizable.

01:14:13.010 --> 01:14:16.450
I think that's a pretty
active area of Investigation.

01:14:16.450 --> 01:14:19.150
For the optimization
part, I mean,

01:14:19.150 --> 01:14:20.480
the sorting isn't that hard.

01:14:20.480 --> 01:14:23.420
I think I just ran it
for seconds on my laptop.

01:14:23.420 --> 01:14:27.430
So you shouldn't
expect greatness.

01:14:27.430 --> 01:14:32.260
I think just to review the
policy gradient framework,

01:14:32.260 --> 01:14:34.270
I think is conceptually clear.

01:14:34.270 --> 01:14:36.770
You write down the expected
reward, you subtract a baseline,

01:14:36.770 --> 01:14:38.420
it's the same thing.

01:14:38.420 --> 01:14:47.800
And then you just take you try
to find estimate of that reward

01:14:47.800 --> 01:14:49.720
or advantage.

01:14:49.720 --> 01:14:52.030
And that's one thing that
we didn't talk about, which

01:14:52.030 --> 01:14:53.673
I think is actually important.

01:14:53.673 --> 01:14:56.090
And maybe next year I should
try to do a bit more of this,

01:14:56.090 --> 01:15:01.630
is that building RL systems and
scaling is actually much, much

01:15:01.630 --> 01:15:04.160
more complicated
than pre-training.

01:15:04.160 --> 01:15:09.120
And the reason is that you have
to do inference and inference

01:15:09.120 --> 01:15:10.810
is its own can of worms.

01:15:10.810 --> 01:15:13.030
You also have to
manage multiple models.

01:15:13.030 --> 01:15:16.380
So you have the policy
you're training, you have--

01:15:16.380 --> 01:15:19.800
if you have PPO, then
you have the critic.

01:15:19.800 --> 01:15:23.550
If you have the
inference and you

01:15:23.550 --> 01:15:25.320
have maybe an inference
worker, then you

01:15:25.320 --> 01:15:28.650
need to ship the model over
there to run inference.

01:15:28.650 --> 01:15:31.150
If you have an
agent environment,

01:15:31.150 --> 01:15:33.970
you have the environment
which requires spinning up.

01:15:33.970 --> 01:15:38.500
And then on top of that, you
also have the pi old and pi ref.

01:15:38.500 --> 01:15:41.020
So you have multiple models
that you need to keep around.

01:15:41.020 --> 01:15:43.440
And on top of that
have to do everything

01:15:43.440 --> 01:15:47.500
in parallel and distributed
and make it all sing and dance.

01:15:47.500 --> 01:15:50.620
So there's a lot
to be done there,

01:15:50.620 --> 01:15:52.860
which we're not really
going to have time

01:15:52.860 --> 01:15:54.040
to talk about in this class.

01:15:54.040 --> 01:15:55.680
But if you're
interested, there's

01:15:55.680 --> 01:15:59.380
definitely pointers
I can send you to.

01:15:59.380 --> 01:16:02.810
So that's it and
see you next time.
